Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 21:23:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 177/209] feat(area): IEditorShapeComponent contract and the
 box that implements it

Sandbox has no way to edit geometry that a component owns: CEntityObject's
bounds and hit test walk entity slots only, so a slotless shape volume is
invisible to framing, to rubber-band selection and to a click, and nothing in
the inspector can start a tool for one component instance.

This is the engine half of the answer (scene-notes/area/decisions/02).
IEditorShapeComponent is a reflected interface base in CryCommon, next to
IEditorEntityComponent and built the same way, so EditorQt can find an
implementation that lives in any DLL with QueryComponentsByInterfaceID. It asks
four questions and no more: local bounds, a world-space ray test with a
viewport-derived tolerance, the class name of the edit tool that owns the
geometry, and that tool's button label. Nothing in it knows what the geometry
is, which is what makes it serve any future component, not only shapes. The ABI
is append-only from here on, because the caller and the implementation are in
different DLLs.

SEntityComponentEditToolTarget travels with it: CEditTool::SetUserData takes one
(key, void*) pair and SEditToolButton calls it exactly once, so the two
identities a tool needs - which editor object, which component instance - go
over in one struct. Both are GUIDs, never raw pointers, so the tool re-resolves
through the managers and can never dereference a deleted object.

IShapeComponent now derives from IEditorShapeComponent rather than from
IEntityComponent directly: a shape is exactly the thing that contract describes,
and one single inheritance chain keeps the static_cast that
IEntity::GetAllComponents performs on its query result correct - a second
IEntityComponent base would make it ambiguous.

CBoxShapeComponent implements both halves. Its editor bounds are its local AABB
in entity space; its hit test tries the twelve wireframe edges within the given
tolerance first, because the previewer draws the box as a wireframe and clicking
a drawn line is what selecting the shape means, and falls back to the slab test
so a click inside a large box still selects it. It also implements
IShapeComponentEdit as a fixed two-point set - minimum and maximum corner - so
that a generic point tool resizes a box without knowing it is a box; a dragged
corner can never collapse the volume below kMinSize.

